(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-coagulation-hemostasis-quiz"]={"title":"Coagulation & Hemostasis Testing Quiz 1 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a low automated platelet count. His smear shows platelet clumps and platelets surrounding neutrophils. What does this indicate?","choices":["A falsely low count","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Immune thrombocytopenia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clumping and platelets ringing neutrophils (satellitism) make the automated count read falsely low, which is spurious thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman is taught the three overlapping stages of hemostasis. Which set is correct?","choices":["Primary hemostasis, secondary hemostasis, thrombosis","Platelet plug, fibrin clot, embolism","Primary hemostasis, secondary hemostasis, fibrinolysis","Coagulation, thrombosis, embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemostasis has three overlapping stages: the platelet plug (primary hemostasis), the fibrin clot built by the coagulation factors (secondary hemostasis) and the breakdown of the clot (fibrinolysis). — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a positive fibrin monomer test. What activity does a positive result indicate?","choices":["Platelet aggregation","Thrombin activity","Vitamin K deficiency","Complete absence of fibrinogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive fibrin monomer test is indicative of thrombin activity and is consistent with intravascular coagulation; severe liver disease and inflammatory disorders can also give a positive result. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with uremia bleeds easily. His platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Which category fits?","choices":["Platelet dysfunction","Factor VII deficiency","Hemophilia A","Hypofibrinogenemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with bleeding suggest platelet dysfunction; uremia is one cause, and platelet function testing is abnormal. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has easy bruising and bleeding gums. Which initial set of studies is appropriate?","choices":["Prothrombin time, activated partial thromboplastin time and D-dimer","Blood count with smear, clotting times and fibrinogen","D-dimer and fibrin monomers only","Bleeding time alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bleeding workup is a blood count with differential and peripheral smear, prothrombin time with international normalized ratio, activated partial thromboplastin time and fibrinogen. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time that corrects when mixed one to one with normal plasma. What does this suggest?","choices":["An inhibitor","A factor deficiency","Lupus anticoagulant","A platelet disorder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Correction on a mixing study suggests a factor deficiency, because normal plasma supplies the missing factor. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a platelet count of 80,000 per microliter (adult reference 140,000 to 400,000). Which term applies?","choices":["Thrombocytosis","A normal count","Pseudothrombocytosis","Thrombocytopenia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A platelet count well below the lower reference limit is thrombocytopenia, here a count under even the 100,000 per microliter figure used to define it. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man undergoes a bedside bleeding time (reference 3 to 10 minutes). At which platelet count is the result meaningful?","choices":["Above 100,000 per microliter","Below 50,000 per microliter","Below 20,000 per microliter","Below 10,000 per microliter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding time is only useful if the platelet count is above 100,000 per microliter, because thrombocytopenia itself lengthens bleeding time. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has a normal platelet count and a bleeding time of 18 minutes (reference 3 to 10). What does this suggest?","choices":["Coagulation factors are deficient","Fibrinolysis is excessive","Platelets are not working well","The platelet count is falsely high"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With a normal platelet count a prolonged bleeding time signals a platelet function problem, a primary hemostasis defect. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man not on warfarin has an international normalized ratio of 2.6 (reference 0.8 to 1.2). Which cause fits?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Liver disease","Hemophilia A","Acute thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Off warfarin, an abnormal prothrombin time or international normalized ratio may indicate a bleeding disorder, a clotting disorder, liver disease or vitamin K deficiency. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has heavy menses and easy bruising. Which inherited bleeding disorder is the most common overall?","choices":["Hemophilia A","von Willebrand disease","Hemophilia B","Factor XIII deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. von Willebrand disease is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, and it is tested with von Willebrand factor antigen, activity and a factor VIII level. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man bleeds, and a physician reviews platelet counts. Which statement about count and bleeding is correct?","choices":["Only low counts cause bleeding","Only high counts cause bleeding","Counts never cause bleeding with a normal prothrombin time","Very low and very high counts can both cause bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thrombocytopenia and extreme thrombocythemia, above 1,000 times 10 to the ninth per liter, can both cause bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with severe malabsorption has a prolonged prothrombin time. Which liver-made protein, dependent on vitamin K, explains this?","choices":["Fibrin","Prothrombin","Plasmin","von Willebrand factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prothrombin is produced by the liver and depends on vitamin K intake and absorption, so vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a prothrombin time ordered. Which pathways does it assess?","choices":["The intrinsic and common pathways","Platelet adhesion and aggregation","The extrinsic and common pathways","Breakdown of fibrin by plasmin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The prothrombin time measures fibrin clot formation after tissue factor is added, so it reflects the extrinsic pathway plus the common pathway factors. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has thrombocytopenia, and her smear shows large, pale gray platelets. Which syndrome fits?","choices":["Gray platelet syndrome","May-Hegglin anomaly","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Spurious thrombocytopenia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrothrombocytopenia with gray platelets on the smear is gray platelet syndrome. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with cirrhosis has easy bruising. Which initial test evaluates his secondary hemostasis?","choices":["D-dimer alone","Bleeding time alone","Prothrombin time with international normalized ratio","Platelet aggregation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Liver disease is an indication for coagulation studies; the liver makes prothrombin, so the prothrombin time and international normalized ratio assess it. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has a prolonged prothrombin time with a low platelet count. Why can one abnormal value not diagnose disseminated intravascular coagulation?","choices":["Other conditions can cause each abnormality","The prothrombin time is always normal in it","Platelet counts rise in it","Only a bleeding time can diagnose it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each abnormality has other causes, for example liver failure, vitamin K deficiency and major blood loss, so the overall picture matters. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has normal prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time, fibrinogen and platelets, and a raised D-dimer. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Acute thrombosis","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Hemophilia A","Platelet dysfunction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A raised D-dimer with normal coagulation studies and platelets is the acute thrombosis pattern, and D-dimer is nonspecific. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with sepsis has a prolonged prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time, low fibrinogen, a raised D-dimer and a low platelet count. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Immune thrombocytopenia","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Vitamin K antagonist effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the pattern of acute disseminated intravascular coagulation: prolonged screens, low fibrinogen and platelets, and a raised D-dimer. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has a negative fibrin monomer test. What does this result mean?","choices":["Intravascular coagulation is excluded","Plasmin activity is absent","The fibrinogen level is normal","Intravascular coagulation is not excluded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A negative fibrin monomer test does not mean intravascular coagulation is absent; a positive one indicates thrombin activity. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a fibrinogen of 8.2 grams per liter (reference 2.0 to 4.0). Which risk is increased?","choices":["Hemorrhage after traumatic surgery","Hemophilia","Platelet dysfunction","Coronary and cerebrovascular disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A fibrinogen above 7.0 grams per liter is a significant risk for both coronary artery and cerebrovascular disease. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a risk assessment for arterial thrombosis. Which two laboratory results are independent risk factors?","choices":["Bleeding time and platelet count","Mixing study and reptilase time","Mean platelet volume and fibrin monomers","Prothrombin time and D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The prothrombin time and D-dimer are independent risk factors for arterial thrombosis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with no bleeding history has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time found before surgery, and a normal prothrombin time. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XII","Factor XI"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Factor XII deficiency prolongs the activated partial thromboplastin time but does not cause bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a low platelet count with a normal prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and platelet function analysis. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","von Willebrand disease","Early liver failure","Immune thrombocytopenia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immune thrombocytopenia, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic uremic syndrome and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia all give a low count with normal clotting times. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man bleeds despite a normal platelet count, platelet function, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time. Which deficiency should be considered?","choices":["Hemophilia A","Factor VII deficiency","Factor XIII deficiency","Heparin effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Factor XIII or alpha-2 antiplasmin deficiency fits: normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with bleeding suggest a platelet disorder, mild von Willebrand disease, factor XIII or alpha-2 antiplasmin deficiency, or impaired fibrinolysis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has a D-dimer above the reference limit of 250 micrograms per liter. What does a positive result confirm?","choices":["Thrombin and plasmin were both generated","A deep vein thrombosis specifically","Platelet dysfunction","A factor VIII deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is produced only by plasmin acting on cross-linked fibrin, so its presence confirms that both thrombin generation and plasmin generation have occurred. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man on heparin has a prolonged thrombin time. Which test stays normal, pointing to heparin rather than a fibrinogen defect?","choices":["Activated partial thromboplastin time","Reptilase time","D-dimer","Mean platelet volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The reptilase time is normal with heparin because it mainly assesses fibrinogen function, separating heparin effect from a fibrinogen problem. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man takes warfarin, and his international normalized ratio is 1.4 (goal 2.0 to 3.0). What is the concern?","choices":["Clot risk","Bleeding risk","Platelet failure","Liver failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On warfarin a low international normalized ratio means too little anticoagulation and a risk of clotting. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man takes aspirin and bruises easily. Which laboratory study directly evaluates platelet function?","choices":["Prothrombin time","Light transmission aggregometry","Thrombin time","D-dimer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Platelet function testing includes light transmission aggregometry, lumiaggregometry, the platelet function analyzer and flow cytometry. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time with low fibrinogen activity and a normal fibrinogen antigen. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Hypofibrinogenemia","Lupus anticoagulant","von Willebrand disease","Dysfibrinogenemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low fibrinogen activity with normal antigen means the fibrinogen is present but does not work, which is dysfibrinogenemia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
